cat > 'README.md' <<'OC_EOF_4'
# OpenCode Cockpit

One calendar workspace and one backend process for running a small team of [OpenCode](https://opencode.ai) agents on your own
machine. You issue one order. A lead model cuts it into sub-orders, other models do the parts, a judge program takes or
refuses what they hand in, and every step is a row in an append-only ledger that the page is drawn from.

- **OpenCode only.** The one agent command this backend ever starts is `opencode run --format json -m <provider/model>`.
- **One model per seat.** Which seat runs which model is a small JSON file. DeepSeek, Kimi and Qwen have their own
  icons; any other model gets a neutral one (see [ICONS.md](ICONS.md)).
- **Nothing to install besides OpenCode.** The backend is Python standard library only; the page is plain HTML, CSS and
  JavaScript with no build step, and it fetches nothing from another host.
- **Local.** The backend listens on `127.0.0.1` and answers only the page it serves.
- **Agent swarms.** Group team members and tasks, then keep handoff notes with the skill versions used. The board, file list, ledger and swarm workspace adapt to phone screens.

## What has and has not been tested

Read this before you rely on it.

- Tested: the dispatcher, the git layer and the earlier cockpit end to end with a **stand-in** for the `opencode`
  executable (`tests/fake_bin/opencode`). The calendar tests use the real request handlers, ledger writer and stand-in
  processes without sockets; they cover dates, history, plan persistence, cancellation, exactly-once issuance, lead-only
  HTTP orders, unchanged ledger bytes on rejection, and each run's saved lead and order issuers.
- The library tests use the same handlers, ledger writer, git blobs and stand-in without sockets. They cover saved
  bytes, refused saves, restarts, historical backfill, launch context, usage receipts, proposals and human review,
  atomic review failure, lessons, context limits, and workflow metrics counted independently from the ledger.
  **Whether a real model plans better or costs less with this library has not been measured.**
- The diary tests (`tests/test_diary.py`) run on a ledger and sender records written by hand, where every expected
  number was worked out by hand, and also drive the real handlers, ledger writer and stand-in without sockets for the
  sender records and `GET /diary`. They cover: one entry per ledger row; refusals and envelopes with no answer; lines
  that are not records; grouping by day and family in fixed zones and in the process's own zone; the ten numbers per
  order; byte-identical repeats; a sender record for every envelope that reached the ledger; `GET /diary` answers and
  errors; and `diary_check.py` exiting 1 on each of 42 damages to a diary (an entry deleted, a number, a seq, an `h` or
  a metric changed, the refused entry dropped, and more). In one stand-in rehearsal (as in "Try it without a model":
  one order and one refused envelope), `diary_check.py` passed the diary with coverage 1. **No diary of a run with a
  real model has been made.** The only no-answer sender record from the backend so far was made by a test, which
  names another ledger in the request.
- The page has static checks and a Node DOM stand-in for routes, both order forms, calendar refresh, links, previews,
  lead identities, sub-order dispatchers, seat order titles, Library filters, file saves, review validation, metrics,
  live state changes, preserved drafts and focused controls, empty states, and flow edges avoiding unrelated nodes.
  Static checks compute text contrast from the CSS tokens and check control states and seat link truncation.
  Browser contracts in `tests/page_geometry.js`, `tests/page_snapshot_checks.js`, and the live/recovery checks
  measure actual geometry, visible count agreement, motion, polling and recovery at 1280 by 900.
  `tests/page_library_loop.js` builds the Library loop through the real page controls and includes a separate
  density fixture: three two-step orders, two proposals per order, and one return.
  `tests/page_home_regressions.js` checks chart size, card count, the completed Home height and proposal placement;
  `tests/page_closing_audit.js` checks rendered spacing, controls, contrast and geometry across all view states.
  `tests/page_library_shelf.js` checks the shared proposal component, six-second review receipts, shelf counts,
  version actions, numbered text and chart/table geometry; the fake agent’s `loop_long` switch exercises long diffs. The completed density assertions are
  Home at most 2980 px and Team at most 3780 px. The proposal band is measured separately while reviews are pending. These scripts run in a locally served browser; they are not
  executed by Python's DOM stand-in. The socket-based cockpit tests require loopback binding.
- The order status. `tests/test_status.py` runs `web/status.js` under Node on hand-made cases: every situation and
  flag, both sides of each boundary (with a whole-second clock a lease with 0 s left has run out; quiet equal to the
  threshold is not too quiet; a last answer exactly two poll periods old is not out of date), a clock with a fraction,
  a holder who is not the order's addressee, ghosts, order ids named after `Object.prototype` members, and the merge
  of `/state` pages. `tests/test_status_scenes.py` builds 17 scenes with the real dispatcher in process, reads them
  back through the real `/state` code, and requires `web/status.js` and an independent Python reading of the same
  rules to give equal cards. `tests/test_diary.py` drives the real `GET /state` handler and checks that it carries the
  sender records with their line numbers, and that `web/status.js` turns a refused issue into a ghost and flags the
  order it was sent under. The same-fact check (who holds an order, how many are not ended, in the chip, the status
  view, Team, Home and the day view) runs in the page harness, over the DOM stand-in. A headless browser was used only
  locally, on a stand-in rehearsal, to read those facts while orders were held and after they ended, and to take
  screenshots. **No real model was called.** Flag 7f uses fixed limits, 120 s for the first row after a grant and
  480 s between rows: the backend sends no rules, so a skill's own interval is never applied.
- **Not tested: a real OpenCode process with a real model.** The command line, the environment switches and the event
  shapes read here follow notes taken on opencode-ai 1.18.32; they have not been checked again against a newer release.
- The model ids and the key variable names in `team.example.json` are examples, not verified values. Run
  `opencode models` to see what your installation calls its models, and check your provider's documentation for the
  name of its key variable.

## Try it without a model

Needs Python 3.9 or newer and git 2.28 or newer.

    python3 cockpit/serve.py ../opencode-runs --opencode tests/fake_bin/opencode

Open <http://127.0.0.1:8800/>, type a title and a goal on Home, and press **Send to lead**. The stand-in plans two sub-orders
and writes one small file for each; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing costs anything.

## One file

`opencode-cockpit.sh` in the repository root holds every other tracked file. Copy that one file to a machine, and

    bash opencode-cockpit.sh [FOLDER]

writes the whole tree into FOLDER (default `opencode-cockpit`) and checks the SHA-256 of each file it wrote. It needs
bash and `sha256sum` or `shasum`; it calls no git and opens no connection. Every file sits in it as a plain
here-document, so it can be read before it is run. It refuses a folder that already holds files and writes nothing
outside FOLDER. The folder it makes is not a git checkout and has no history; running the cockpit there still needs
Python and git as above.

The file is generated: after changing any tracked file run `python3 tools/make_oneshot.py`, or `tests/test_oneshot.py`
fails. That test runs the script into a temporary folder and compares every file. The script has been run with bash 3.2
on macOS only; it has not been tried on Linux or any other system.

## Run it with OpenCode

1. Install OpenCode and make sure `opencode` is on your `PATH` (or pass `--opencode /path/to/opencode`).
2. Copy `team.example.json` to `team.json` and put in the models you want, written `provider/model`.
3. Export your provider keys in the shell that starts the backend, and list the *names* of those variables under
   `pass_env` in the team file. Keys are never written to a file by this project.
4. Start it:

       python3 cockpit/serve.py ../opencode-runs --team team.json

Choose a runs root outside this source repository. Every start makes a new folder under that root (ledger, git
repository, sandboxes, logs). The backend refuses a root inside this repository so its shared library cannot enter
the source tree. Ctrl-C stops the backend; an
agent that is still running then is ended and what it produced is not collected.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--team FILE` | `team.example.json` | which seat runs which model |
| `--port N` | `8800` | the port on `127.0.0.1` |
| `--max-launches N` | `12` | how many agent processes one start may run in all; the first refusal stops everything after it |
| `--wall SECONDS` | `600` | one agent process is ended after this long (a rate-limited model makes `opencode run` hang silently) |
| `--judge FILE` | the floor judge | a judge of your own (see below) |
| `--opencode PATH` | `$OPENCODE_BIN`, else `opencode` | the executable to start |
| `--skill-registry PATH` | optional | also list skills from this shared registry JSON file in the swarm workspace |
| `--swarm-board DIR` | optional | show the roster and recent main-channel posts from a local swarm blackboard |
| `--clock-offset SECONDS` | `0` | shift the shared ledger, run-name and local calendar clock for rehearsals; negative values work too |

Each order is also limited to two launches. Model cost is whatever OpenCode reports in its `step_finish` events; the
launch table shows it, and this project sets no spending limit of its own beyond the launch counts.

## Using the page

### Compare release quality

Open **Quality radar** in the cockpit footer for the measured release snapshot. Select an
indicator cell to inspect its raw observation, bound, direction and evidence. The twelve
axes report measured means and separate coverage counts. Gray cells have no measurement.

For a new edition, keep the preceding report, exercise an isolated local fixture with the
same team and orders, and use one configuration file per edition. Each config identifies
the slot (`paid`, `gpt` or `mine`), human-readable `revision`, absolute source `root`,
local running `url`, and optional `paid_url`, `skills`, `installer`, `copycheck`,
`copy_roots`, `run_dir`, `legacy_radar` and `mutation_root`. Set `git_root` and the exact
`commit` for history checks. Missing optional tools leave evidence gaps. For a paid
frontend with a separate dispatcher, set `app_root` to the backend checkout. For cross-version visual comparisons, supply the same local `visual_fixture_url` and
private `visual_cache_dir` for every edition; snapshots then replay the same read-only data
while each edition serves its own assets. Source control and the private measurement
toolkit stay on the machine running the checks.

    python3 tools/make_oneshot.py --candidate /tmp/cockpit-candidate.sh
    python3 tools/quality.py measure edition.json --fixture release-review-v1 --out edition-sample.json
    python3 tools/quality.py build pro-sample.json edition-sample.json original-sample.json --out next.json
    python3 tools/quality.py seal previous.json next.json --slot gpt --out web/quality-data.json
    python3 tools/make_oneshot.py

Run the measurement command once for each edition being measured. Keep the generated samples
and the previous report outside the source tree. A candidate installer is for measurement.
The normal packer verifies the sealed scores against the frozen rubric, the previous values
and the runtime files. Advancement requires a higher previously measured deterministic score,
preserves previous measurements and deterministic scores, and keeps performance checks that
already met their thresholds. To audit a saved report, run `python3 tools/quality.py check REPORT`.

### Organize an agent swarm

Open **Swarms** from the sidebar or the Team section on Home. You can also bookmark
`http://127.0.0.1:8800/#/swarms`. The private Pro build has the same workspace at `/pro/#view=swarms`.
Pick a run, create a named swarm, choose its members and link the orders or sub-orders they coordinate.
Open a linked task to inspect its hand-ins and ledger. Use the swarm journal to record a decision or handoff
against a member, a task and a specific skill version. These workspace edits take effect immediately and persist
across restarts. Dispatching work still goes through the existing order form.

The skill catalog includes cockpit Library skills and can also read a shared skill library. Pass
`--skill-registry /path/to/skills/registry.json` when starting the server. Existing run records are available
in the run picker for review. The current run accepts new groups, edits and journal entries.

To follow a team already using a shared blackboard, pass `--swarm-board /path/to/.swarm`. The current
run shows its members and the latest 20 regular posts from the main channel in a read-only panel.
The swarm journal is stored with the cockpit run and records the title and version of each cited skill.

Shortcuts outside text fields: `n` focuses the Home order title; `g` then `h`, `c`, `b`, `t`, `s`, `f`, or `l` opens
Home, Calendar, Board, Team, Swarms, Files, or Ledger. `l` alone opens Library; `?` opens Help. Cmd/Ctrl+Enter sends
the Home form. Escape closes a file preview, seat drawer or confirmation and returns focus to its opener.

The Library loop in five steps:

1. Send an order and wait for accepted files.
2. Keep a useful file as a skill or reference, editing its title or tags if needed.
3. Send another order; the lead receives available entries and may name those it used.
4. Review a proposed revision: Accept or Decline, with six seconds to Undo.
5. Use the accepted version in another order and inspect its recorded outcomes.


Home shows today's orders, those of the live run that have not ended (an open order of a stopped run reads "Past run,
stopped"), what needs attention (including every order the status warns about), and the live run's launch count. Type a title
and goal in its compose card, then press **Send to lead** or Cmd/Ctrl+Enter. The order goes to the named lead with
the same default write scope, budget of six, and judge rules as today's day form. An empty title or goal keeps the
draft and explains what is missing. While sending, the button is busy and a second submission is ignored. Success
keeps Home open, clears the submitted fields and shows a five-second confirmation with an Open order link.

The Team row on Home shows each seat's model, working or idle state, launch count, and last order.
Six seat colours follow the backend's seat list, lead first, and stay fixed across drawings, filters and polling;
teams larger than six reuse those slots in list order, while You and Judge remain neutral.
Card headings and the four hero tiles have original tinted icons, with a consistent wash for each subject.
White drawing surfaces have small shadows; links and controls have hover and keyboard-focus states.
Cards, curves and bars enter once per navigation; working dots and running stripes keep their phase across polls,
and the page honours changes to the system's reduced-motion preference by disabling animations while keeping the short control-state transitions.
One shared seat-state rule supplies the same **Working** or **Idle** word to Home, the reporting tree, Seats cards
and the selected seat panel. Any active launch makes its seat Working, even if a newer launch has already ended.
Recent activity groups up to 12 ledger messages into sender bubbles, newest first, with order links, times and
recipient chips after each sentence. Group headers name the sender; each bubble starts with a verb and ends with a
separate clock time. Choose Everyone or a sender above the conversation; that selection survives polling.

The run is also drawn in these views:

- **Home:** a visible lavender, cyan and apricot hero band; its four white stat tiles include explanatory counts.
  The activity curve counts order-related ledger rows in 32 equal time buckets, using the same eligibility rule as
  Recent activity (including questions resolved to an order). Organisation, pool and unrelated rows do not count.
  Dots sit on the curve at order endings, alongside succeeded, waiting and failed order counts. Its clock axis spans
  at least 60 seconds, with five clock labels; the smooth filled curve stays above its baseline. Before any eligible
  rows arrive, only the baseline and an empty-state explanation appear.
- **Home / Results:** accepted top-level orders sent by You in the live run, out of all such sent orders, with a progress
  bar and the latest acceptance's order link and time. Up to five currently accepted files from those orders and their
  descendants appear newest hand-in first, with the recorded author, revision and size when available. File links open
  their orders. No file rows appear before a sent order is accepted.
- **Home / Today's orders:** All, Running, Succeeded and Needs attention filter chips show counts and retain selection through
  polling. Running includes waiting and judging; Needs attention includes failed, dead and cancelled orders requiring a decision. An unmatched filter leaves
  a compact explanation in the same card; All restores every order.
- **Home, board and order:** progress bars count succeeded direct sub-orders, or the order itself as one step when
  it has no children. The bar uses the order’s running, succeeded or failed state colour.
- **Team / Who reports to whom:** You, the lead, all working seats and the judge, connected as a reporting tree.
  Select the lead or a worker to dim unrelated nodes and rows. The original focus panel within the tree card shows its model,
  reporting and directing relationships with icons, tiles for launches, launched seconds, distinct file paths handed
  in and sent-back launches when present. Its launches appear newest first, with order links, start times, seconds,
  process states and the first line of the result (the full result remains in its tooltip). Up to 12 of its own messages
  follow, newest first. Missing launch timestamps and durations are labelled as unavailable.
  Select it again or choose **Show everyone** to clear focus. Native buttons support Tab, Enter and Space, and
  selection and existing controls survive polling without toggling their focus attributes.
- **Team / Levels:** beside Models in use, four filled isometric slabs accompany You, Lead, Working seats and Judge
  program from top to bottom. Each has a top and two side faces, with one blue hue darkening down the stack; a working
  slab uses the accent colour. White-ringed discs count every member and name them in tooltips. Thin leaders join
  slabs to their rows, which show population, working count and launches at the same height. Larger teams get more
  room for member marks, with drawing and rows growing together. Working means an active launch, or at least one
  order awaiting Judge's check; Judge is counted once, not once per order.
- **Team / Who handed what to whom:** each sub-order's recorded sender and recipient, with its state and every
  file path handed in for it. Tiles and cards have 12-pixel gaps; each seat shows sent and received counts.
  Connections share one trunk and bus per sender, with a drop to every card. The lowest bus is 16 pixels above
  the cards, and other senders’ buses are spaced 8 pixels higher.
- **Team and order / When each launch ran:** one bar per launch. Team uses seconds from the run’s start; an order uses
  seconds from its first launch through its last end (or the latest backend observation while running). Overlapping
  launches get separate lanes within their seat. A striped end marks a launch still running.
- **Order / How the team ran it:** directly below the timeline, one card per drawn launch follows its seat-row and
  start-time order. Each shows the seat icon and name, recorded model, order link, process state, seconds, recorded
  tool calls and distinct files handed in. Sent-back launches add that count; token categories and cost appear only
  for positive recorded values. The muted result shows its first line, with the full result in its tooltip. The
  existing launches table remains below, including launches whose timing is unavailable.
- **Team / Models in use:** seats, launch counts and measured launched seconds grouped by model, with time-share bars.
- **Order / Run at a glance:** elapsed order duration, launches, sent-back count, handed-in files and direct sub-orders.
  The tiles share the full row width, whether there are four or five. The existing three workflow metrics remain
  under the order title with their original values.
- **Empty states:** Nothing needs your attention has a tray and check, No future plans has a calendar page, and empty
  board columns have a dashed card outline. These original 56-pixel inline drawings use two shared colour tokens.

Team now includes a searchable canvas with every seat and live order, selection, drag/arrow-key panning and
30–150% zoom in 10% steps. Its Timeline, Events and Files tabs share the selection with the full seat panel below: conversation
filters, a draft sent through the existing lead-only order endpoint, and expandable launch steps. Search and canvas
selection leave the existing focus/dimming controls independent; focusing a seat also selects it on the canvas.
Unchanged polls retain all workspace nodes, drafts, open steps, tabs and transforms. Levels and Models share a row.
Fit centres the graph at the largest step that clears the stage edges and bottom-right toolbar; graphs too large at 30% centre on the selected node and remain pannable.

Replay the canvas with the slider or Play at four times real speed (one-second steps every 250 ms with reduced motion), seeing recorded order states, launch spans, events, files and totals at that time.
A historical position stays fixed through polls while the seat panel stays live; Latest returns the canvas to the current run and resumes following updates.

`/team.ledger_updated` is the timestamp of the last ledger row, or null for an empty ledger. Alongside `run_started`,
it measures first-to-last ledger time without waiting for all `/state` pages and does not advance on idle polls.
Conversation recipients use explicit destinations first, then recorded submitters/lease holders for verdicts and
reporting relations for progress and hand-ins; submitted reports go to the configured judge. Launch steps use lease
tokens and sequence boundaries to separate retries. Missing launch timing or file authorship is labelled unavailable.

The reporting tree, hand-off diagram and order flow use rounded 2-pixel blue connectors (`--edge: #1f6fe5`), with blue arrowheads in the flow, white-filled 3.5-radius blue rings at both ends of every team connector, and dashes distinguishing judge and prerequisite lines.

Drawings include captions, fact tooltips and empty states. An old launch with no recoverable timestamps stays in
the launch table with an explanation beside its timeline; its position is never invented. All drawings are local
inline SVG or positioned HTML, with no chart library or additional request to another host.

The order page follows its state, sub-orders and seats as they change. Its metrics show launches, sent-back work
and seconds. While work is running these are estimates from its launches (seconds sum the durations reported so
far); a finished top-level order uses its stored workflow metrics, whose seconds measure elapsed time from issue
to completion. File rows keep the preview action. Press **Keep as skill** for reusable instructions or **Keep as
reference** for material a later order can consult. The row changes to a Library link immediately. Files from
sub-orders can also be kept from their parent's page, with the file's own source order preserved.

Open **Library** in the left navigation to see every entry, including rejected and superseded versions. Filter by
skill, artifact, workflow or lesson. Pending proposals appear first; an entry opens its kept text, version, usage,
source order, replacement link, and any workflow steps or lesson reason. A proposed skill has an immediately
visible review bar: **Accept skill** makes it available to future launches; **Reject proposal** requires a reason
and records that reason as a lesson. A proposal reaches no seat before a person accepts it.
Past-run order pages stay read-only; **Keep in the Library** opens a Library form for the file's title, kind and optional tags.

The Library's **What each order cost** chart and table list finished orders from oldest to newest, with launches,
sent-back counts, elapsed seconds and what the lead used. A dot and the word "library" mark reuse in the chart.
An order's own Library block links to what the lead used and to proposals awaiting review. These measures make
reuse and changes in launch counts visible; they do not establish that a real model is better or cheaper.

Views refresh without replacing unchanged data. Existing controls are reconciled in place to keep drafts,
selection and focus; initial loads show skeleton blocks. The day form keeps its existing behavior: after sending,
it stays on that day and offers an **Open order** link.

## Calendar and planning

Open the calendar icon in the top bar, then choose a day. Today can issue an order immediately; a later day can save
a plan; an earlier day's form is closed. A plan becomes due on its date, but **only pressing Issue now starts its
order**. It never starts just because time passed. Waiting and due plans can be cancelled.

History spans every run folder under the same runs root. Orders belong to the local day of their issue event; day
totals count top-level orders, and launch totals include their sub-orders. Open an order from any view to see the same
flow, models, launches, files and ledger. Past runs are read-only. Unreadable run ledgers are reported, not hidden.
Each new run pins its team configuration in `team.json`; older runs can use their saved `logs/team.json`.

**Every order goes through the lead.** A person's order goes to the running team's lead, who plans the parts and hands
them to the other seats; the judge decides whether their work passes. `POST /message` rejects an `order.issue` unless
`body.to` is that lead actor, returning 400 `{"cli": "not-through-lead", "detail": "..."}` before the dispatcher sees it,
with the ledger bytes unchanged. Other envelope kinds and sub-orders issued internally from the lead's plan keep
their existing behavior. The day form and order cards identify the lead by name, model and icon.

Plans live in the append-only `planned.jsonl` at the runs root, outside all run ledgers. Issuance uses the same
`order.issue` envelope and ledger writer as the day form, under the server lock. Repeating an issue request returns
the existing run and order; recovery also checks for an order written before an interrupted plan receipt.

The left rail opens Home, Calendar, Board, Team, Files, Ledger and Library. Home lists what needs attention, today's
orders, the team and upcoming planned days. The board spans all runs. Team, Files and Ledger show the live run. The question
mark opens Help, whose examples fill today's form without sending it. Hash routes support Back and Forward.

Home now pairs activity with a launch ring and acceptance meters, adds a Monday-to-Sunday week strip, per-order step segments and seat stacks, six Library picks, and six newest file hand-ins beside a bounded conversation with Show all. Pending proposals appear in a full-width review band above the charts; the side attention card is for orders.
Team adds six stat tiles and shares tinted seat headers, launch sparklines and four measured seat totals with Home; seat, state and Library-kind chips carry their own readable ink and wash.
Replay keeps whole-run node positions, connector routes and rail order fixed, timelines label each seat once, and ambiguous launch file totals explicitly say “files in this order”; unchanged polls preserve nodes, drafts, selections and inner scroll positions.

The endpoints use the same host and origin gate as `/message`:

| Endpoint | Result |
|---|---|
| `GET /state` | the live ledger and folded state, and `sent`: the live run's sender records, each with `n`, its line number |
| `GET /team` | live seats, models, launches and files; `seats[].outcomes: {accepted, sent_back}`; `order_totals: {orders, accepted, steps, steps_accepted, all_accepted, sent_back}`; `files[].by`, `at` (epoch seconds), `seq` |
| `GET /history` | server `today`, `live_run`, runs, days with orders/plans/stats, each order's `lead`, `steps: [{id, title, state}]` (direct children in plan order, or the leaf itself), `participants` (worked seats with configured colour `slot`), `week` (seven server-local dates), and skipped runs with reasons |
| `GET /order?run=RUN&id=ORDER` | `lead`, order fields, direct children, descendant flow nodes, seats, launches, files and ledger; orders include `by`; seats include whole-run `outcomes`, and files include `by`, `at`, `seq` |
| `GET /diary?run=RUN&day=YYYY-MM-DD` | one day of a run's diary (see below); `run` defaults to the live run, `day` to the backend's today; a bad date returns 400 `bad-query` |
| `GET /file?path=KEY&run=RUN` | handed-in file preview; omitting `run` selects the live run |
| `POST /message` | a dispatcher envelope; the day form sends `order.issue` |
| `POST /plan` | save `{title, goal, writes, budget, day}` for a real future date; returns `{id, state: "waiting"}` |
| `POST /plan/issue` | `{id}` issues a due plan; returns its run and order; repeats return 409 `already-issued`, waiting returns 409 `not-due` |
| `POST /plan/cancel` | `{id}` cancels a waiting or due plan; closed plans return 409 |

Invalid plans return 400 `bad-plan`. Unknown runs or orders return 404. Run ids must identify a direct folder under
the runs root; traversal and symlinks are refused. File previews resolve ledger artifact keys, never arbitrary disk
paths. The page refreshes state and history every second, using the backend's local date rather than the browser's date.

`GET /order` includes `lead: {actor, name, model, family}` from that run's saved team, and `by` on `order`, every
`children` item and every flow node, taken from its `order.issued` ledger row. Each day's `orders` items in
`GET /history` carry the same `lead` object for their own run, even when the live team has changed. If a historical
run has no saved team, the lead fields are empty strings; `GET /history` reports unreadable team records in `skipped`.

The drawing fields are additive. `/team` includes `run_started` (the first ledger timestamp) and `handoffs`, whose
items contain `order`, `parent`, `from`, `to`, `title`, `state` and `files` (distinct handed-in paths in ledger order).
Launches include `started` and nullable `ended` timestamps: a finished process ends at its recorded start plus its
measured `secs`; a running one has no end yet. `/order` normalizes those timestamps for older launch records and
adds `run_started`, `updated`, and `timing: {started, ended, seconds}` for the order's issue-to-ending duration.
Unfinished live durations use the backend clock; unfinished past runs stop at their last recorded observation.
`/team` also includes `seats[].id`, the configured seat id used by focus buttons. `/history` adds
`days[date].orders[].progress: {total, succeeded}`; `/order` adds the same object on `order`, every `children` item,
and every `nodes` item. `total` counts direct children and `succeeded` counts those in the succeeded state. When
there are no children, `total` is 1 and `succeeded` is 1 only if the order itself succeeded. Seat panel totals and
activity buckets are calculated from the existing backend launch records and ledger rows; no counts are invented.
Results, Levels, stat notes and seat launch details also use these existing fields: root `order.issued` rows identify
orders sent by You, `/state` supplies current success states, `order.succeeded` rows supply acceptance times, and
`artifact.rev` rows supply file authors and hand-in order. File authors are matched to the exact `/team.files` revision;
when revision metadata is unavailable, the newest artifact row for the path is used. Levels use configured actors and
active launches; sent-back seat totals count that seat's launches with a failing recorded verdict. Launch cards use
`launches[].secs` or `started`/`ended`/`updated`, sum `seen.tools`, count distinct `taken` paths and use `verdict` for
sent-back work. Optional numbers come from positive `seen.tokens` categories and `seen.cost`. Tool and file counts
count reported entries; a running launch can show zero before its results are collected. These launch-card measures use existing fields; the additional Home and Team fields are listed above.
Seat outcomes count distinct orders, including steps; later acceptance retains a prior send-back, and repeated failures count once per seat. All display durations use recorded seconds or backend timestamps, never the browser clock.
No fields were removed from `/state`, `/team`, `/history` or `/order`.

## The status and the diary

The **status** says, from the live ledger alone, where every order stands: not ended, held under a valid lease,
waiting for the judge, or needing attention. The page polls `GET /state?since=` for the rows it has not seen yet and
for the sender records, which show an issue the dispatcher refused or never answered and the last refusal about an
order; the header chip names what each number counts, and one click opens one card per order (`#/status`).

The **diary** tells a run in sentences. `cockpit/diary.py` writes one entry per ledger row and one per envelope the
dispatcher refused or never answered, grouped by local day and by top order. Every entry carries a pointer to the line
it was made from: a ledger row is its `seq` and the row's `h`; a sender record is its line number in `sent.jsonl` and
the first 16 hex digits of the sha256 of that line. Each order also gets ten numbers: seconds until it was taken, from
being taken to its first word, the longest silence while held, whether it passed the first time, refusals by code,
envelopes with no answer, expired leases, launches, how often it was sent back, and seconds from issue to its end.
`GET /diary` works a day out on request; the same inputs give the same bytes. Days and times are in the zone the
backend runs in (set `TZ` in the shell that starts it), the same zone the calendar uses.

The **sender records** are `sent.jsonl` in each run folder. The backend appends one JSON line for every envelope it hands
to the dispatcher (the lead loop, the agents through the backend, the page and issued plans). Each line has the
envelope's `id`, `from` and `kind`, `at` (the dispatcher's clock), the `order` it is about and the order it was sent
`under`, then the answer: `ok`, `code`, `detail` and the ledger rows it wrote (`events`). `ok` is false for a refusal,
with the dispatcher's `code` and `detail`, and null when no answer came back (`detail` then says why). An order sent to someone other than
the lead is refused before it reaches the dispatcher, so it has no sender record. Run folders made before this file
existed have no sender records; their diary has ledger entries only.

To write a diary to files and check it against the raw files:

    python3 cockpit/diary.py RUNS/RUN --out /tmp/diary          # diary.json and diary.md; --tz +08:00 for a fixed zone
    python3 cockpit/diary_check.py RUNS/RUN --diary /tmp/diary/diary.json

`diary_check.py` shares no code with `diary.py`. It works every entry and number out again and prints one JSON line
`{entries, no_pointer, mismatch, coverage, rows_total, rows_cited, refused, no_answer, metrics_mismatch, ok}`; it exits 0
only when every pointer resolves, every ledger row is cited (`coverage` 1), the refused and unanswered counts match the
sender records, and every order's numbers are its own.

## The library and the lead's loop

The shared library lives at `<runs root>/library/`, outside the source repository and outside every run. It survives
restarts and is available to later runs using the same root. It is not listed as a run. An atomic snapshot stores
entry metadata, original bytes and processing receipts together; a filesystem lock serializes writers from different
runs. The library adds no model calls.

- A person can keep any file listed by `GET /order` as an **artifact** (reference material) or a **skill** (UTF-8
  instructions for seats). Both are accepted immediately. Saving the same run, order, path and kind again returns
  the original entry. Its `sha256` and `bytes` describe the exact handed-in blob, even if a later order changed that
  path. Tags are text strings; titles have 1 to 200 characters. Artifact previews decode binary data with replacement
  characters, but the stored bytes and digest remain exact.
- The backend records one **workflow** per finished top-level order, including success, failure, exhausted leases
  and cancellation. It backfills older runs on startup. Steps contain all descendant sub-orders in issue order.
  Metrics count `lease.granted` rows in the family, leases beyond the first for each order (`sent_back`), and seconds
  from issue to the terminal ledger event. `sent_back` also counts retries unrelated to a judge's refusal.
- A **lesson** preserves each judge refusal or terminal failure, its order, seat and reason. Exhausted leases get a
  backend explanation because there are no judge words for that event. Rejecting a proposal also records a lesson.

Every seat receives `_dispatch/LIBRARY.json`, even when empty. Its `v` is `library/1`; it contains accepted skills
with their text, accepted artifact summaries and sources, the 10 newest workflows, and the 20 newest lessons. Only
the newest accepted skill in each replacement chain is included. Proposed, rejected and superseded entries never
reach seats. The entire JSON document is at most 64 KB: oldest entries are dropped first across all kinds, as whole
entries. A skill too large to fit is kept on disk but omitted from context. The dispatch channel never counts as
handed-in work or as an out-of-scope change.

The lead's rules require it to read the library before planning, reuse what fits, and name entry ids in the optional
`uses` array beside `orders` in `_dispatch/plan.json`. Known ids count once per accepted plan, with duplicate ids
ignored; later replanning can count another use. Unknown ids are retained in the workflow's `unknown_uses`, but do
not increment a counter. Workers are told to follow the listed skills that fit their order.

The lead may also include `propose`, a list of up to three objects:

    {"kind": "skill", "title": "Review checklist", "text": "Check the inputs before writing.",
     "replaces": null, "why": "Avoid repeat launches."}

Each valid object among the first three becomes a proposed skill, attributed to the lead actor and its order.
Text must be nonempty UTF-8 of at most 8 KB. A replacement must name an existing skill and gets its version plus one;
a new skill starts at version one. Malformed proposals are ignored without changing how the git layer judges the
plan. Only a plan that issued sub-orders contributes uses or proposals. A person must accept a proposal before it
reaches seats; accepting supersedes its named predecessor. Rejecting requires a nonempty reason.

All library routes use the existing host and origin gate. Errors include `cli` and `detail`.

| Endpoint | Result |
|---|---|
| `GET /library` | `{entries, counts, popular}`; entries stay newest first; `popular` contains up to six entries ordered by usage, then newest timestamp and creation sequence; counts for artifact, skill, workflow and lesson |
| `GET /library/item?id=ID` | Entry plus `text`, at most 64 KB; 404 `unknown-entry` |
| `POST /library/save` | `{run, order, path, kind, title, tags}` returns `{entry}`; kind is artifact or skill |
| `POST /library/review` | `{id, decision, reason}` returns `{entry}`; decision is accept or reject; only proposals are reviewable |
| `GET /library/context` | Exactly the document the next launch receives, provided no intervening library change occurs |
| `GET /library/efficiency` | `{orders: [{run, order, title, ended, state, launches, sent_back, seconds, uses}]}`, oldest completion first |

`GET /order` adds `library: {workflow, uses, proposed, saved}`. `workflow` is its workflow id or null; `uses` contains
`{id, kind, title}` objects; `proposed` contains pending entries from the order's family; `saved` maps file paths to
saved entry ids. When a path has both kinds, the most recently created saved entry is shown there; both remain in
the library. Times are UTC ISO strings, and ids are readable kind/title/counter slugs of at most 48 characters.

A save accepts only a file listed for that run and order (including its descendants). Traversal, absolute paths,
unknown runs and files belonging only to another order return 400 `not-a-handed-in-file`, with nothing written.
Reviewing a non-proposal returns 400 `not-reviewable`; rejecting without a reason returns 400 `reason-needed`.

For deterministic rehearsals, the stand-in accepts `FAKE_OPENCODE_LIBRARY`, a JSON object with `uses` and `propose`
fields, when its name is in the team's `pass_env`. Setting `uses` to `"context"` names every entry it receives.
Adding `"loop": true` makes an order with “improve” in its title propose a changed version of the first available
skill plus one deliberately weak proposal. This switch is confined to the stand-in executable.
With the variable unset, its existing two-sub-order behavior stays unchanged. These tests measure the plumbing,
not the quality or cost of a real model's plans.

The keep buttons prefill a title and file-type tag; an inline disclosure allows edits before the one-click save.
An optional `revision` on `/library/save` rejects a stale file with 409 rather than keeping a revision the person
has not reviewed. `/order.library.saved_revisions` maps `path@revision` to the kept entry; the older `saved` map
remains. `/library.available` is the skill/artifact subset actually included by the bounded launch context.
Order summaries add `library_uses`, and each entry adds `usage`, `last_used`, `pending`, `author` and `search_text`.

The page's Decline sends `decision: "decline"` with an optional reason; the older `reject` contract still requires
one. Accept and Decline return a six-second undo token. `/library/undo` restores the previous accepted version or
marks the decline lesson undone, retaining the review audit trail. `/library/item` includes the full version
lineage, and `/library/diff?from=<id>&to=<id>` compares two versions in that lineage. The per-version table and
chart show observed order outcomes, launches and elapsed seconds; they do not claim that reuse caused an outcome.
Search covers title, tags and stored text, with kind/tag filters and newest/most-used ordering. Press `l` for
Library; `g` then `l` still opens Ledger.

## The team file

    {
      "lead":  {"id": "lead", "name": "Lead", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-chat"},
      "seats": [
        {"id": "writer",   "name": "Writer",   "model": "moonshotai/kimi-k2"},
        {"id": "reviewer", "name": "Reviewer", "model": "alibaba/qwen3-coder-plus"}
      ],
      "pass_env": ["DEEPSEEK_API_KEY", "MOONSHOT_API_KEY", "DASHSCOPE_API_KEY"],
      "permission": {}
    }

- `lead` sits in the planner seat: it may only hand in a list of sub-orders, and any file it writes is left where it is.
- `seats` are the workers. Each gets one OpenCode process per order, started with its own model.
- `pass_env` names the environment variables an agent may see. Everything else in your shell is kept from it.
- `permission` is merged into the OpenCode permission config the agent is started with. A non-interactive
  `opencode run` cannot ask a person, so use `allow` or `deny`, never `ask`.

## What happens to an order

1. Issuing an order sends one `order.issue` envelope to the lead. Saving or cancelling a future plan writes only to the separate plan file.
2. The lead gets a plain folder holding `_dispatch/ORDER.json` (the order, the rules of its seat, the team, the budget)
   and `_dispatch/LIBRARY.json`, and writes `_dispatch/plan.json`: the sub-orders, who gets each, what each may write
   and read, which waits for which, and optionally library uses and skill proposals.
3. Each worker gets a plain folder of its own with no `.git` in it. Only files inside the order's write scope are
   taken; anything else stays in that folder and is listed on the launch (`launches.jsonl` in the run folder, `GET /team`).
4. The judge named on the order rules on what was handed in. Accepted work is merged in the run's git repository and
   tagged under `accepted/`; refused work goes back with the reason, at most once more.
5. When all sub-orders have passed, the lead's order is judged and closed.

The default judge (`cockpit/judge_floor.py`) holds only a floor: something was handed in where the order said, no file
is empty, and each file reads as what its name claims (text is UTF-8, JSON parses, Python compiles; nothing is run).
**It does not look at whether the work is any good.** To hold more than that, write a file with the same three
functions and pass it with `--judge`.

## Safety boundaries, and their limits

- An agent's environment is rebuilt from a short allow-list plus `pass_env`; auto-update and session sharing are
  switched off; the two OpenCode rules that would ask a person are set to `deny`.
- An agent works in a folder of its own, and what it changes outside that folder inside the run folder is recorded on
  its launch. **This is not a sandbox:** an agent that is allowed to run shell commands can reach whatever your user
  account can. Run it in a container or a spare account if that matters.
- The page never inserts text as HTML, and the backend serves five named files and no other path on disk.

## The pro page (private)

`pro/` holds a second page for the same backend, served at <http://127.0.0.1:8800/pro/> (the header links to it when
the folder is there). It is built on a paid UI kit that the owner licensed, and its text is in Chinese. **The kit's
licence does not allow publishing it: `pro/` must stay in a private repository.** The one-file script never carries
it (`tools/make_oneshot.py` leaves out `pro/`, and `tests/test_oneshot.py` checks that), and `tools/privacy_scan.py`
allows the paid-kit and not-English classes under `pro/` only.

- `pro/dist/` is the built page, committed, so the backend serves it without Node. It talks to the backend it was
  served from (same origin), so live status and sending work without `?backend=`.
- `pro/data/` holds the programs that write the page's data files (`events.js`, `audit.js`, `know.js`, `head.json`,
  `hq.json`, `hq.head.json`, `diary.json`, `moments.json`, `raw/`) from a run folder. `cockpit/pro_data.py` runs them
  for the live run into a temporary folder when the ledger, the launches or the sender records changed since the last
  pass (checked on each request for a data file), and removes that folder when the backend stops.
- To rebuild after changing `pro/src/` (needs Node 20 or newer):

      cd pro && npm install && npx vite build

  The kit folder in `pro/` holds only the kit items the build uses; `vite.config.mjs` resolves `@/components/ui/<name>` there,
  and a copy in `pro/src/kit4/<name>/` wins over the original. `pro/shell.html` (the light shell and the blue
  recolouring) is put into `dist/index.html` after the build. Commit `pro/dist/` again after a rebuild.

## Tests

    for t in tests/test_*.py; do python3 -B "$t" || break; done
    node --check web/app.js
    python3 tools/make_icons.py --check
    python3 tools/make_oneshot.py --check
    python3 tools/privacy_scan.py

`tests/test_page.py` also runs `tests/page_harness.js` when Node is installed. This uses a DOM stand-in, not a browser
or a rendering engine. `tests/test_calendar.py` and `tests/test_library.py` use temporary runs roots outside the
source repository and open no sockets.

`tools/privacy_scan.py` looks through the tracked files (and, with `--history`, the whole git history) for home-folder paths, the local
account name, addresses, keys, session links, AI attribution lines, text that is not English, and picture metadata, and
exits 1 on any hit. It reads text, not pictures, so it cannot see what a screenshot shows.

## Layout

    cockpit/     dispatcher.py (the ledger and its rules), gitws.py (git layer and sandboxes), live.py and serve.py (the
                 running backend), history.py (read-only run replay), planning.py (human-triggered plans),
                 library.py (shared entries, context and ledger-derived learning records),
                 diary.py and diary_check.py (a run's diary and its independent check),
                 opencode_agent.py (the one place a process is started), judge_floor.py, schema/,
                 pro_data.py (the pro page's data files for the live run)
    web/         index.html, app.css, app.js, status.js, icons.js (generated)
    icons/       the three SVG sources and their licence
    tests/       unit tests including test_calendar.py, page_harness.js (DOM stand-in), a simulator, and the stand-in executable
    tools/       make_icons.py, make_oneshot.py, privacy_scan.py
    pro/         the private pro page: src/, the kit items it uses, data/ (its data writers), dist/ (built); see above
    opencode-cockpit.sh   every other tracked file in one bash file (generated; see "One file")

## Licence

MIT, see [LICENSE](LICENSE). The three model icons are from another MIT project and their logos are trademarks of
their owners; see [ICONS.md](ICONS.md). This project is not affiliated with OpenCode or with any model provider.

The Library leads with current accepted entries, then proposals waiting for review, then complete compact history.
Display titles derived from file headings do not rewrite stored titles or bytes. Repeated lessons share a history
heading while keeping every record and source link. Entry pages show version history, numbered scrollable text,
and observed per-version outcomes. Review cards stay available for six seconds of Undo before leaving; reduced
motion removes the exit animation. “Use in a new order” prefills the form and leaves sending to the person.
