## Spec interpretations

- G5 says all duration layers floor, but durationSec.1 explicitly requires `duration(Math.round(sec * 1000))`. The specific durationSec rule wins; every later duration layer floors.
- Fractional truncation limits are not defined; valid limits are floored to an integer grapheme budget. Invalid/subminimum limits use the specified default.
- Invalid clamp bounds are not defined; nonnumeric/NaN bounds default to zero, numeric infinite bounds remain bounds. Defined behavior for invalid n, reversed bounds and infinite n follows the spec.
- A fractional badge maximum is compared as supplied, then displayed with count; the spec only specifies invalid/sub-one maxima, not integer normalization.
- toMs uses built-in Date parsing as requested. Offset-free date strings therefore retain JavaScript's parsing semantics; relative date functions themselves never read a clock or timezone. App-generated relative labels pass explicit backend time and UTC.
- The spec permits environments without Intl.Segmenter. The fallback counts Unicode code points; supported Node/browser environments use full grapheme segmentation. No polyfill or dependency was added.

